junban — November 2026:
Modified for junban in 2026 by junban contributors.
Derived from task-spooler 1.0.4. Added foreground execution, strict FIFO CPU-slot
scheduling, process-group lifetime tracking, per-user queue locking, estimates,
agent-readable listings and wait notifications, a terminal watch view, and macOS
idle-sleep prevention. Removed the old command-line interface and obsolete
packaging, output capture, mail, dependencies, and queue-reordering features.

v0.5.0 (scheduling by slack):
 - Replaces EASY backfilling, which protected only the first waiting job
   and protected it to the second. Now every waiting job has slack, a
   fifth of the time given with --estimate, and a job starts ahead of
   others only if that costs each waiting job no more than the slack it
   has left. The cost is how much later junban's plan then starts the
   job, so however many jobs start ahead of it, a job is delayed by no
   more than its slack. A job started without -c and a job without an
   estimate have no slack.
 - A job that uses the GPU lines up ahead of the waiting jobs that can
   run beside it, so the GPU is no longer left idle behind CPU jobs. They
   give up only the CPUs of one GPU job. It stays behind earlier GPU jobs
   and behind a job that cannot run beside it, such as one started
   without -c. This needs no estimates. junban ls lists the waiting jobs
   in the order of the line, so job IDs there are no longer in order.
 - A job that waits only for the GPU no longer keeps CPUs free: the job
   that holds the GPU gives its CPUs back with it.
 - junban counts only on what it was told: a job without an estimate may
   run for good. It starts ahead of a waiting job only if all jobs
   without an estimate together leave that job what it asks for. While a
   waiting job waits for one of them to end, jobs with an estimate may
   start ahead of it, unless it was started without -c: nothing starts
   ahead of such a job while its start is unknown. With no estimates at
   all, CPU jobs keep their order as before. Tags and directories never
   change the order.
 - A running job past its estimate is expected to end a minute later,
   then 15, 30 and 60 minutes later, and so on, instead of at once, and
   the server wakes at those times. This uses up no one's slack.
 - Slack that has been used is kept in the server only and starts over
   after a live switch. No protocol or state change, so a 0.4.0 server
   switches to this version in place.

v0.4.0 (live upgrade):
 - After junban is upgraded, the running server switches to the new
   junban the next time any junban command connects. It notices that the
   junban it was started as (for example /opt/homebrew/bin/junban) has
   been replaced, saves its state to an unlinked file, and execs the new
   junban with the listening socket, the lock and every client connection
   still open. The process ID stays, and running and queued jobs,
   finished results and job IDs are kept.
 - The server first asks the new junban, with the internal --live-check,
   whether it speaks the same protocol and reads the same state layout.
   If not, the server keeps running the old junban, and junban shutdown
   is needed as before.
 - Servers before 0.4.0 cannot switch, so stop one with junban shutdown
   once after upgrading. No protocol change.

v0.3.1 (completes EASY backfilling):
 - A job with an estimate may also start ahead of waiting jobs if it
   uses only CPUs and GPUs that the first waiting job leaves free once it
   can start, by the estimates of the running jobs. Such a job may run
   past that time without delaying the first waiting job. 0.3.0 had only
   the other half of EASY backfilling, so a two-CPU GPU job waited behind
   a 14-CPU job even though the GPU and two CPUs would stay free.
 - Jobs without an estimate still wait their turn.
 - No protocol change.

v0.3.0 (backfilling with estimates):
 - A job with an estimate may start ahead of waiting jobs in the free
   CPUs and GPUs if, by its estimate, it ends before the first waiting
   job could start. That time comes from the estimates of the running
   jobs; a job past its estimate counts as ending at once, and nothing
   goes ahead while the first waiting job needs what a running job
   without an estimate holds. Jobs without an estimate keep their turn,
   so with no estimates the order is as before.
 - run --help and the READMEs say that an estimate lets a job start
   early and should not be shorter than expected.
 - No protocol change; a running 0.2.x server keeps working, but keeps
   the old order until it is restarted.

v0.2.1 (watch layout fixes):
 - watch sizes the directory column to the names on screen, up to 32
   columns, so names over 16 columns are no longer cut early. Space goes
   to the directory first, then the CPUs and GPUs, then the timing, and
   the tag last. Jobs only counted in "... N more" take no room.
 - Queued jobs show their estimate next to the time waited.
 - The one-row table for terminals under 64 columns is gone. Narrow
   terminals use the same two-row cards: the tag is dropped first, then
   the timing shrinks, and each piece on the second row shows whole or
   not at all.
 - No protocol change; a running 0.2.0 server keeps working.

v0.2.0 (adds GPUs as a second resource; changes ls and notice output):
 - junban run -g N (--gpus N) gives the GPUs a job uses: 0, the default,
   or 1. The GPU is one device; only one -g 1 job runs at a time. -c and
   -g do not change each other's default, so -g 1 alone still takes all
   CPUs. A GPU job that needs little CPU gives both, as -c 1 -g 1.
 - Scheduling is FIFO for each resource. A waiting job reserves the CPUs
   and GPUs it asks for; a later job starts only within what is left. A
   CPU job may pass a job that waits only for the GPU, and a job that
   needs all CPUs still keeps everything behind it waiting.
 - ls shows gpus=N on every line, ls --summary and watch show
   gpus BUSY/TOTAL, and the narrow watch table has a GPUS column. The
   wait notice says gpus=N and whether the job waits for cpus, gpus or
   both.
 - watch shows CPU and GPU gauges and gives each job two rows: its ID and
   full command, then its directory (blue, ending in /), timing with a
   progress bar against the estimate, the CPUs and GPUs it holds, and its
   tag. Finished jobs show a check or a cross, the result and the clock
   time they ended. Colour marks meaning only, NO_COLOR turns it off, and
   a non-UTF-8 locale gets ASCII, with "?" for each non-ASCII character in
   job text. Terminals under 64 columns keep the one-row table. ls still
   cuts the command at 80 bytes.
 - The protocol version is now 736, so stop a running older server with
   junban shutdown after upgrading.

v0.1.1 (changes junban ls output and exit status for scripts):
 - junban ls prints only job lines on standard output, each starting with
   running, queued or done. An empty queue prints nothing. The summary line
   "cpus B/T running N queued N" moved to junban ls --summary. (#3)
 - junban ls exits with 1 when it lists no job (it used to exit with 0).
   It exits with 0 when it lists a job and 125 on junban errors. (#4)
 - junban ls takes --id N and --dir NAME to list only some jobs. (#5)
 - Jobs can carry a tag: junban run --tag TAG, or JUNBAN_TAG in the
   environment. ls shows it as tag=TAG and takes --tag; junban kill --tag
   stops all jobs with a tag. The protocol version is now 734, so a running
   older server must be stopped with junban shutdown (which still works
   across versions) after upgrading. (#6)
 - junban run --detach queues the job, prints only its ID and returns. The
   job's output goes to <queue socket>.logs/ID.log, shown as log= in ls. (#2)
 - junban wait [-v] [--timeout DUR] [--tag TAG]... [ID...] blocks until jobs
   finish. It exits with their highest exit status, 124 on timeout and 125
   on junban errors. The server now keeps the results of the last 1000
   finished jobs for it; ls -a still shows the newest 20. (#1)
 - dir= keeps printable UTF-8 such as Japanese directory names instead of
   writing them as %XX; control and format characters, Unicode whitespace,
   % and invalid bytes are still encoded. junban watch measures columns in
   terminal cells, using tables generated from Unicode data. (#10)

## Features to be implemented

Doubtful tasks:
 - Allow the 'only-tail' output, without any storage. The number of lines
   or bytes should be choosable.
   - No program should give that big amount of output, so it cannot be stored.
 - What happens if the output disk is full? Should be decide a good behaviour
   in that situation?
   - It's up to the running program; ts gives the descsriptor to it.

Future:
 - Use a better system than mkstemp() for finding output files, so we can add
   .gz to the gzipped outputs.
